  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 121 AM MDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nRidging will amplify over The Great Basin on Wednesday, with the \ncentroid of the ridge setting up over the Four Corners. A shortwave \nmoving into the High Plains of Colorado Wednesday afternoon will \nsend a backdoor front southward into eastern New Mexico Wednesday \nnight, replenishing moisture east of the central mountain chain. \nThis will push PWATs slightly above normal (per GEFS and EPS \nguidance) and translate to a slightly more active day Thursday, with \nstorm coverage highest along the central mountain chain. Recycled \nmoisture will make Friday a repeat of Thursday, with outflow \nboundaries determining exactly where moisture pools.\n\nThe Four Corners high will slowly shift eastward late week, \ncentering itself around the NM/CO border. The high will also be \nquite strong, with 500 mb heights 594 to 598 dam over New Mexico. So \ndespite near average moisture, storm coverage will be on the low \nside, with isolated to scattered storms and a relatively low risk of \nflash flooding.\n\nLong-term guidance is in good agreement that the ridge will continue \nslowly moving eastward into Texas and Oklahoma next weekend. This \nwill place southerly flow aloft over New Mexico and open up the \nregion to the advection of sub-tropical moisture. The main moisture \nplume will likely remain off to the west over California and \nArizona, but it looks like western NM could be in the fringes of \nthis moisture plume by next weekend. Temperatures may cool a few \ndegrees across the west next weekend, but central and eastern areas \nwill remain hot with well-above normal highs.",
